WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.013
- Today we're doing a cookie episode.

00:00:02.150 --> 00:00:03.663
- Yes, cookies.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:07.043
Gotta start things off right
and get some cookie gravy,

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:08.590
AKA milk.
- Milk.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.480
I may be slightly lactose intolerant.

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.030
- Not cookie intolerant, right?

00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:14.193
- No, no.
- I'll drink milk to that.

00:00:14.364 --> 00:00:16.530
(playful music)

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.130
Okay, I can't hide this anymore.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.510
Adam messed up and instead of milk

00:00:20.510 --> 00:00:21.800
we have drinkable yogurt.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:24.150
It's so thick.
- Today on Worth It,

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.450
we're gonna be trying three cookies

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:26.850
at three drastically
different price points

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.640
to find out which one is the
most worth it at its price.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:30.473
- Nice.

00:00:30.473 --> 00:00:31.590
- What is the best time to eat a cookie?

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.070
- Cookies are really an any time food.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:35.340
I would eat a cookie before bed,

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.393
after bed, in the middle of the day.

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:39.030
- So we're on our way
to Mah-ze-dahr Bakery,

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.153
having shortbread cookie.
- Also you still

00:00:40.153 --> 00:00:42.203
have milk on your mouth.
- It's yogurt.

00:00:43.078 --> 00:00:44.510
(upbeat music)

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:47.010
- My name is Umber Ahmad,
this is Mah-ze-dahr Bakery,

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:48.950
and today we're trying
our vanilla shortbread.

00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:51.030
- Where does the name
Mah-ze-dahr come from?

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:52.560
- [Umber] It's a word
in the Urdu langugage

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.470
to describe the magic or the essence

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:55.550
that makes something special.

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:57.550
It's what draws you in again and again,

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.770
and we hope that that's
what you find with our food;

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:01.480
you fall in love with
us and you come back.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.250
We were named Best
Donut in New York City--

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:04.160
- Really?
- By the New York Times.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.800
And last year we were put on
another New York Times list

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.350
of the 10 Best Foods of the Year.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:10.250
- So you have all these
fantastic pastries,

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.913
I wonder why is it important to still have

00:01:11.913 --> 00:01:13.990
that shortbread at a dollar?

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:14.867
- We don't want anyone
to come into the bakery

00:01:14.867 --> 00:01:17.103
and think that they
can't afford to be here.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.960
There're certain things
that cost a lot more money,

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.270
and there's bite-sized indulgences,

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.478
and that's what a shortbread is.

00:01:21.478 --> 00:01:23.090
- [Andrew] Does it come in a
couple different flavors, or?

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:24.480
- [Umber] It does, buttered pecan,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.130
and we also do a chocolate sable.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:28.180
- [Andrew] And what are
the defining flavors

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:29.480
or essence of shortbread?

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:31.810
- It kind of shatters a little
bit, which is really fun.

00:01:31.810 --> 00:01:34.710
The most important thing, you
can't hide behind shortbread.

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.630
It is what it is, totally naked.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.460
So we start with the
highest quality ingredients,

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:41.310
high fat butter, unbleached
flour, Madagascar vanilla.

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.650
Erin Kanagy-Loux is our
executive pastry chef,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.260
and she's going to be
working with me today

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.150
to make the shortbread.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.490
Once we've made the dough,
we run it through a sheeter

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:50.710
and we punch the cookies
out, sprinkle sugar on them,

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:53.410
and then we put them in a
325 degree convection oven.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:54.770
We tend to underbake them

00:01:54.770 --> 00:01:56.740
and then watch the last couple of minutes.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.960
Mah-ze-dahr Bakery was
born of my love of food.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.290
As a child, my family
traveled all over the world.

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:03.140
We'd be in Sweden, and we'd
have bread for breakfast,

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:04.310
and then we'd have to close our eyes.

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:05.960
My mom would say tell us what you taste.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.360
It would have saffron in it and I'd say,

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:08.940
God this tastes like the rice in Spain.

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:10.650
Food became a way to connect people

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:12.350
through flavors and experiences.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:14.830
- Do you still cover your
eyes and eat food, or?

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.450
- I do, I don't cover
my eyes in restaurants,

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:18.410
but my team tells me often that
when I'm tasting something,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.360
I'll close my eyes.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.400
- We should do that.
- Close our eyes and taste it?

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.233
- Yes.
- Ooh.

00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:23.133
- Whoa.
- Thank you.

00:02:23.133 --> 00:02:23.966
- Cheers.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:26.790
(jazzy music)

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:30.280
- Oh man that smells--
- Like butter?

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:31.390
- Yeah, like butter.
(Steven laughs)

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:32.223
- Cheers.

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:34.939
(funky music)

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:39.240
I can taste the butter, very present,

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.040
a slight hint of vanilla.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.070
- It is the perfect drink
accompaniment cookie.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.283
- Yes.
- And just nibble away,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.440
not worried about getting
crumbs on yourself.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.360
- Oh, I got a lot of
crumbs on myself though.

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.660
- Adam, could we get more?

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:53.550
- A bite?
- Can I have another?

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.263
Yeah.
- I want another one.

00:02:56.263 --> 00:02:57.096
- I need another, Adam.

00:02:57.096 --> 00:02:58.330
Now that's what I'm talking about.

00:02:58.330 --> 00:03:00.417
This is when you get comped at the casino

00:03:00.417 --> 00:03:02.883
and they bring over just
a tray of chips for you.

00:03:03.787 --> 00:03:05.035
- Wow.
- Chocolate cheers.

00:03:05.035 --> 00:03:07.535
(funky music)

00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:10.940
(Andrew laughing)

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.860
- I cannot eat with my eyes closed.

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:13.950
- You know what this tastes like?

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:15.420
When you get a little bite

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.440
of the still-crystallized cocoa powder?

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:18.690
That's my mah-ze-dahr moment.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:20.740
When I close my eyes and eat this cookie.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.310
Just come in from playing in the snow,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:24.900
my fingers are still a little numb,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:27.703
I'm drinking my cocoa,
and at the end of the cup.

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:30.240
- It's very weird to watch people eat

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.073
with their eyes closed.

00:03:31.073 --> 00:03:32.980
- Really?
- I'm just realizing that now.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.310
- Also the smell of this
cookie is a bakery at work.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.763
- If this was a cologne,
I would irresistible.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:42.883
- Sure, but it will never be.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:46.090
You could eat this cookie in any outfit.

00:03:46.090 --> 00:03:47.850
Like this cookie is perfectly at home

00:03:47.850 --> 00:03:49.820
wearing a tuxedo after the opera

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:51.780
or flip-flops and shorts on the weekend.

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:53.290
You could take this cookie to the ball,

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:55.163
you could take this cookie to the beach.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.100
- This is my just woke up
at 1:00 a.m. in my boxers,

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.360
I just wanna eat a cookie.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.083
- I take back the outfit thing.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:05.050
You in your boxers eating this
cookie is unacceptable to me.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.150
- Fine, I'll put on a shirt--
- Unacceptable.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:08.283
- Before I eat this.
- Sure, thank you.

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.140
- Can I feed you?

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:13.303
Okay.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.510
That shortbread cookie--
- Yes.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:17.463
- So good.

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:18.973
- It was very good.

00:04:19.064 --> 00:04:20.647
When you think of--

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:25.460
We're getting spoiled today, guys.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.590
- Usually sweets before a meal

00:04:27.590 --> 00:04:29.360
is not the right way to go,

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.700
but cookies are one of those things

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:33.810
where I don't mind making
them my pre-game meal.

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:36.750
- I mean cookies' the
perfect afternoon dessert.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.220
The like four o'clock cookie--
- Yeah.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:39.660
- Is truly a thing to behold.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:42.743
- So the next cookie is
actually pretty much a meal.

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:46.540
- Yes, I think it was
invented to be calorific.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:47.530
- Calorific.

00:04:47.530 --> 00:04:48.363
Anyway--
- So.

00:04:48.363 --> 00:04:49.860
Yeah it's gonna be a day full of cookies,

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:51.320
but cookies are an any time food, so--

00:04:51.320 --> 00:04:52.853
- That's right.
- No big deal.

00:04:52.853 --> 00:04:54.810
(jazzy music)

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.348
- Hi, I'm Connie McDonald.
- My name is Pam Weekes.

00:04:57.348 --> 00:04:58.890
We're at Levain Bakery
and you're gonna be trying

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.520
our world famous cookies today.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.840
- So you've known each other for--

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:02.673
- Very long time.
- Yeah.

00:05:02.673 --> 00:05:04.190
- Can you tell us about how you met?

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:06.620
- We were actually training
for an Ironman triathlon.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:07.590
- [Connie] And sharing the cookies

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.060
just like made it all the more fun.

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:11.335
- Was the cookie an after bike ride thing?

00:05:11.335 --> 00:05:12.168
Was it in your bib--
- After?

00:05:12.168 --> 00:05:13.440
- during the bike ride?
- Both.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:14.810
- [Andrew] Did you ever
take it on the ride?

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:15.643
- Yeah.

00:05:15.643 --> 00:05:17.130
You know sustainable
and often durable enough

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.030
where you can just put it
in your bike shirt and go.

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:20.970
- How many different types
of cookies do you have here?

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:22.870
- [Pam] Chocolate chip
walnut, oatmeal raisin,

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.190
a dark chocolate chocolate,

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:25.550
and a dark chocolate peanut butter chip.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:26.930
- [Connie] With the
chocolate chip walnut cookie,

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:28.460
it's creaming butter and sugar,

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.980
adding eggs, flour, salt, leavening,

00:05:30.980 --> 00:05:32.750
semi-sweet chocolate chips, and walnuts.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:34.360
It's just like really good ingredients.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:35.193
So simple.
- We try to keep

00:05:35.193 --> 00:05:36.770
things simple.
- So when you

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:37.603
first baked these cookies,

00:05:37.603 --> 00:05:39.823
what was the motivation for the style?

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.070
- [Pam] The size gives
us the crunchy outside,

00:05:42.070 --> 00:05:44.660
the gooey inside.
- So it's less about scale

00:05:44.660 --> 00:05:46.620
and more about textural effect.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:47.630
- Exactly.
- 'Cause I've heard

00:05:47.630 --> 00:05:49.150
a lot of people say like oh Levain,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:50.510
that's the giant cookie place,

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:52.880
but no it's the place where
it's the perfect combination

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:53.840
of textures.
- Yeah.

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:55.864
- That's nice, I like that.
- Put that on a T-shirt.

00:05:55.864 --> 00:05:57.635
- [Pam] Yeah.
(people laughing)

00:05:57.635 --> 00:05:58.468
(jazzy music)

00:05:58.468 --> 00:06:00.063
- Ooh, feel how heavy this is.
- Can I?

00:06:00.063 --> 00:06:02.800
This is like one child.
- Yeah.

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.513
We've gotten every type of cookie.

00:06:04.720 --> 00:06:06.540
This should be the chocolate chip walnut.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.803
Check out how far apart my fingers are.

00:06:08.822 --> 00:06:10.300
- I can't wait to see the inside.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:11.800
Oh, that's gooey.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.610
Look how many chocolate chips are in here.

00:06:13.610 --> 00:06:15.093
This is crazy.
- Yeah.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:18.300
(jazzy piano music)

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:20.880
- I can't talk 'cause my mouth is so full.

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:22.013
- I need milk again.

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:24.660
- You get so much in one bite,

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:27.833
crunchy, crispy, gooey.
- Ooh-eey.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.233
- Ooh-eey.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:31.250
Not Ew-eey gooey 'cause it's not ew.

00:06:31.420 --> 00:06:34.403
- No it's more like ooh-ee that's gooey.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.390
The walnut really does do something

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:38.920
for the chocolate chip cookie

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:40.343
that I wasn't expecting.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.360
It smells like good chocolate.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.810
Is that stupid to say?
- No.

00:06:47.041 --> 00:06:49.330
(jazzy music)

00:06:49.330 --> 00:06:51.920
I was expecting it to be too chocolatey.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:53.470
- Yeah, it's really not, it's very good.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:55.660
- When is the best time
to eat this Levain cookie?

00:06:55.660 --> 00:06:57.410
- I would eat this cookie any time.

00:06:57.510 --> 00:06:58.870
- Any time?
- Yeah.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:00.420
When would you eat this cookie?

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.410
- I guess you're right actually.

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:02.610
- Tell me a time that you
wouldn't eat this cookie.

00:07:02.610 --> 00:07:04.260
- Like, I would eat it right now.

00:07:04.395 --> 00:07:05.228
You know?
- That's a good answer, yeah.

00:07:05.228 --> 00:07:06.270
- You know what I mean?
- You think about it

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:07.670
and now you want it.
- Exactly.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.503
- [Andrew] Okay now we'll do the oatmeal.

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.490
- It never gets old how
heavy this cookie is.

00:07:12.490 --> 00:07:14.523
- No, it feels like a good meatball.

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:18.240
- That might be the one.
- People sneeze

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:20.943
at the oatmeal raisin, I
think they're sleeping on it.

00:07:20.962 --> 00:07:21.840
- [Steven] You know what I just realized?

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.210
- That we've eaten three cookies

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.113
and now I need to go do a triathlon?

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.640
- The most tempting thing
when you're making cookies

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:28.610
is to eat the cookie dough.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:30.703
You're getting that texture, too,

00:07:30.810 --> 00:07:31.780
in the middle of the cookie.

00:07:31.780 --> 00:07:34.070
- I've never really seen the
appeal of raw cookie dough.

00:07:34.070 --> 00:07:35.370
- What's wrong with you?
- I think

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.740
for the first time I'm
getting that satisfaction

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:39.633
in a way that I find acceptable.

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.170
- Worth It cookie fact, we're
not doing a fact today, sorry.

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.570
- For this next part,
we're gonna be traveling

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.173
to Japan again.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.323
- We made it!

00:07:52.323 --> 00:07:53.530
What a ride, huh?

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.090
Today we're in Tokyo and we brought

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:56.933
our very best friend, Rie McClenny.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.000
- Hello.
- Andrew found a

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:02.000
amazing cookie, maybe one
of the best in the world.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:04.660
- So this is a very
unique style of cookie.

00:08:04.660 --> 00:08:07.290
It's focused on the visuals, the artistry,

00:08:07.290 --> 00:08:09.100
and it's also extremely unique

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.070
because we're not going to learn anything

00:08:11.070 --> 00:08:13.000
about the person who makes it.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.830
They would prefer that we just
focus on the cookie itself

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:18.790
without the influence of
information about them.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:21.350
- I can't wait, this is
a very first on Worth It.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:22.720
- I'm so excited.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:23.860
They're not even gonna let us know

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:26.070
where they work, what they do,

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.173
it could be a Bat Cave, it
could be a Fortress of Solitude.

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:33.093
- No interview, but we're
gonna now cut to something.

00:08:33.472 --> 00:08:36.222
(dramatic music)

00:08:46.330 --> 00:08:48.360
- [Shane] Would you look
at these beautiful cookies?

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:51.030
These are the work of
Antolpo Icing Cookie,

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.343
a custom cookie maker in Japan.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:55.070
So Andrew said you're not gonna learn

00:08:55.070 --> 00:08:56.860
anything about the people
behind these cookies,

00:08:56.860 --> 00:08:59.170
and that's true we're not gonna
get an on-camera interview,

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:02.123
but we can let you in
on a few cookie secrets.

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:04.770
The cookie you're looking
at now is about $90.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:06.830
It's a custom design, and it's based

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:08.763
on a traditional Japanese motif.

00:09:08.780 --> 00:09:10.640
Shipping cookies obviously prevents them

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:12.730
from getting too fancy with their designs,

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.730
but luckily since they're
making a custom one

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:17.440
for us here that's going
to be devoured immediately,

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:19.870
they had the chance to be a
little more ornate with it.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:22.070
It takes this one person hours

00:09:22.070 --> 00:09:25.243
of painstakingly detailed
work to make this cookie.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:27.150
Each little layer of frosting has to dry

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.580
before they can apply another one.

00:09:28.580 --> 00:09:30.823
So yeah, that's where the $90 comes from.

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.490
Sure, plenty of cookies are cute,

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:35.503
but Antolpo strives to
create elegant cookies.

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:37.750
In their process of making iced cookies,

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:40.190
Antolpo discovered you could use the icing

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:42.360
to create three-dimensional designs.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:44.040
And the ultimate use of that technique

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.750
is creating a bird with
individual feathers,

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:48.333
each with their own depth.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.280
- Before we get to the main feature,

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.600
we're going to have one of
Antolpo's smaller offerings.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:57.510
I don't believe that
there's a cookie in there.

00:09:57.510 --> 00:09:59.850
This looks like how jewelry is sold.

00:09:59.850 --> 00:10:02.000
This reminds me of the
packaging from Lego.

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:04.153
I can't destroy this, Adam!

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:06.023
Don't make me eat it, you eat it.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:07.270
No, we'll eat it.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:09.020
Okay, it's fine it's just a cookie.

00:10:09.123 --> 00:10:11.706
(gentle music)

00:10:11.720 --> 00:10:13.253
I can't believe I just did that.

00:10:13.253 --> 00:10:15.836
(gentle music)

00:10:16.570 --> 00:10:17.540
- It's awesome.
- Yeah.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:18.373
- It's not too sweet.
- No.

00:10:18.373 --> 00:10:19.503
- I like that a lot.

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.700
- Can I finish this?

00:10:22.700 --> 00:10:23.533
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:10:26.258 --> 00:10:28.091
- That was a big bite.

00:10:30.187 --> 00:10:32.583
- Are you speaking Japanese or English?

00:10:32.827 --> 00:10:33.660
(Andrew laughing)

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.453
- It tastes very nostalgic to me.

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:36.533
- Oh.
- Really?

00:10:36.533 --> 00:10:38.763
- It's like something my mom made.

00:10:38.782 --> 00:10:40.723
- Why do you think that is?
- Cookie?

00:10:41.138 --> 00:10:44.138
(everyone laughing)

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:45.919
- Every time I look at this, I smile.

00:10:45.919 --> 00:10:46.940
- It's crazy!
- When I look at pictures

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:48.240
of it, I smile.
- Look!

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:49.910
Can we do head for scale real quick?

00:10:49.910 --> 00:10:50.743
- Yeah.

00:10:52.830 --> 00:10:54.920
It's not just impressive in this scale.

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:57.490
- Oh yeah.
- It's like a topographic map.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:10:58.410
- Yeah.
- You can see

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:01.490
the Rocky Mountain as it
spills out into the Pacific.

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.140
- Death Valley down here by the legs.

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:04.190
- Yeah, look at that.

00:11:04.200 --> 00:11:05.940
- Can you tell that we're just trying

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:07.949
to avoid eating this? (laughs)
- Yeah.

00:11:07.949 --> 00:11:09.980
I don't wanna do it.
- Where do you even start?

00:11:09.980 --> 00:11:11.060
- [Andrew] Let's start with the feet.

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.493
Three, two, one.

00:11:16.190 --> 00:11:17.623
- I felt so bad doing that.

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:19.500
I'm sorry.
- Cheers, Steven.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:20.333
- Cheers.

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:25.750
- Very reminiscent of the shortbread.

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:27.250
- I like this one more than the last one,

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:28.953
'cause it's bigger.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:30.630
- Oh God now I want--
- I know.

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.330
- Now I got the taste in my mouth.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:34.200
(laughs) I went to break off more.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:37.140
You know how you're supposed
to leave a cookie for Santa?

00:11:37.140 --> 00:11:39.990
- [Steven] Can you imagine
Christmas Eve at Antolpo's house?

00:11:39.990 --> 00:11:41.700
- What would he do when
he came down the chimney?

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:44.250
He was like, this?

00:11:44.250 --> 00:11:46.510
I thought I had the little
elves in my workshop.

00:11:46.510 --> 00:11:49.113
They've been out making
cookies for someone else!

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:50.880
Wanna eat the tail section?
- No!

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:51.935
- Really?
- I wanna keep--

00:11:51.935 --> 00:11:54.080
- I wanna eat it.
(Steven gasping)

00:11:54.080 --> 00:11:55.600
It's like a little angel's wing.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:56.590
- You murderer!

00:11:56.590 --> 00:11:59.830
This cookie is literally just
four ingredients with icing.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:02.940
You have this elegant design
with this very simple food,

00:12:02.940 --> 00:12:04.803
which is what makes it so great.

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:06.990
Yeah, Rie agrees with me, which means

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:08.690
I know I'm saying something right.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.010
What was that?
- I just wanted

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:11.947
to feel the texture in my mouth.

00:12:11.947 --> 00:12:13.653
- Oh, okay.
- It's so awesome.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:15.073
Yeah it feels so nice.

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:18.833
I love this cookie.
- Okay.

00:12:19.370 --> 00:12:21.120
Let's let Adam and Annie have some.

00:12:21.665 --> 00:12:24.332
(playful music)

00:12:25.905 --> 00:12:27.674
- I can't do it.
- So weak.

00:12:27.674 --> 00:12:30.341
(playful music)

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:40.850
- Which cookie was the most
worth it to you, Andrew?

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:42.070
Rie I don't think you get one,

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:43.420
because you only had one of them.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:45.220
- But my Worth It Winner is Antolpo.

00:12:45.967 --> 00:12:47.114
(laughing)

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:48.783
- Alright, Rie gets one.
- You didn't

00:12:48.783 --> 00:12:49.616
eat the other one.

00:12:49.616 --> 00:12:51.560
- I agree actually,
Antolpo's my Worth It Winner.

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:52.753
- Really?
- Look,

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:55.440
something that makes me not want to eat it

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:58.200
but is also delicious,
what is that thing called

00:12:58.200 --> 00:13:00.550
when you have like your
cognitive dissonance?

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:02.603
Cookie-niv-nitive dissonance.

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:04.650
- Who to pick, who to pick?

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.973
Antolpo, a feast for the eyes,

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:09.219
Levain a feast for my thighs.

00:13:09.219 --> 00:13:10.480
(laughing)

00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:12.050
My Worth It Winner, Mah-ze-dahr.

00:13:12.050 --> 00:13:15.270
It was the best taste per net weight

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:17.070
of any of the cookies we had.

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:19.440
And that's a criteria
that I'm going to choose

00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:21.480
to value the most because all the cookies

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.460
were genuinely so delicious.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.930
It's the mah-ze-dahr, you know?

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:26.930
I can't put my finger on it.
- That's fair.

00:13:26.930 --> 00:13:28.640
- You weren't there,
but mah-ze-dahr is like

00:13:28.640 --> 00:13:31.255
the je ne sais quoi.
- The magic--

00:13:31.255 --> 00:13:32.350
- I don't understand French.

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:36.163
- Is there a Japanese
phrase for secret sauce?

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:37.263
- Secret sauce?

00:13:37.916 --> 00:13:40.424
(speaking foreign language)

00:13:40.424 --> 00:13:42.480
I think it's called a hidden flavor.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:44.075
- What was that?
(speaking foreign language)

00:13:44.075 --> 00:13:44.908
Who said it better?

00:13:44.908 --> 00:13:47.640
- Well say that again.
(speaking foreign language)

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:50.563
- What?
(Andrew laughing)

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.970
Annie, who's your Worth It Winner?

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:55.080
- Wow.
- That's right.

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.533
And, finally, Adam?

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:02.280
Next up, what are we gonna see, Andrew?

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:04.130
- We're gonna see egg-shaped things,

00:14:04.130 --> 00:14:06.700
fish-shaped things, box-shaped things.

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:07.533
What does that mean?

00:14:07.533 --> 00:14:09.490
You don't know.
- If you could just rhyme,

00:14:09.490 --> 00:14:13.200
You could be Dr. Seuss Two.
- I would be Dr. Zeus,

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:15.750
'cause that's how good I
would be at rhyming at it.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:20.910
C-A-L-O-R-I-E-S, we gettin' calories.

00:14:25.050 --> 00:14:26.353
- [Steven] Oh yes.

